<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="oleate biosynthesis I (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5147" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: OLEATE-CPD Oleate| (oleic acid) is the first unsaturated fatty acid that is generated from the saturated fatty acids produced by the fatty acid synthase. It makes up 55-80% of olive oil (although the free acid may consist only 0.5-2.5%) and 15-20% of grape seed oil and sea buckthorn oil. Oleate synthesis occurs with membrane-bound systems. The desaturase enzymes that produce oleate have been isolated from fungi, yeast, and mammalian liver and found to be specific for |FRAME: STEAROYL-COA|. In plants, on the other hand, oleate is produced via |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs|, an acyl-carrier-protein bound intermediate |CITS: [4831331]|. In most plants, the primary products of fatty acid biosynthesis (|FRAME: PWY-5971|) are |FRAME: Palmitoyl-ACPs &quot;palmitoyl-[acp]&quot;| (16:0-ACP) and |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs &quot;stearoyl-[acp]&quot;| (18:0-ACP). The enzyme |FRAME: CPLX-7072|, which is ubiquitous in plants, introduces the first double bond in |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs &quot;stearoyl-[acp]&quot;| while still bound to the ACP protein. The oleate product can either be utilized within the plastid for lipid assembly and further desaturation, or exported to allow further desaturation in the endoplasmic reticulum, as well as incorporation into lipids. The fatty acids that are exported out of the chloroplast are first cleaved from ACP by thioesterases, followed by activation to fatty acyl-CoAs by an outer envelope |FRAME: AT1G77590| activity. Once activated to acyl-CoAs, they are exported to the endoplasmic reticulum |CITS: [14973169]|. The incorporation into lipids occurs via the |FRAME: TRIGLSYN-PWY &quot;Kennedy pathway&quot;| or via alternative, acyl-CoA-independent enzymes such as |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.158| (PDAT) |CITS: [10829075]| or |FRAME: MONOMER-16590| (DGTA) activities |CITS: [Stobart97]| (see also |FRAME: PWY-782| and |FRAME: PWY-762|). Other desaturases that insert double bonds in positions other than the &amp;delta;9 position of 18:0-ACP, have been reported. However, |FRAME: STEARIC_ACID| is the only fatty acid that is desaturated in its ACP-bound form. All other desaturation reactions occur only after the fatty acid has been attached to a glycerolipid backbone. Unusual unsaturated fatty acids are mostly found in seed storage lipids.</Description>
